<a href="https://festivitas.app">Festivitas</a> is a fun little holiday-themed Mac app that developer Simon Støvring introduced two years ago and has been updating since. Its main purpose is to add lights to the Mac's Dock and menu bar, but there's also a version for the iPhone and <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/roundup/ipad/">iPad</a>.<br/>
<br/>
<img src="https://images.macrumors.com/article-new/2026/11/festivitas.jpg" alt="" width="1005" height="656" class="aligncenter size-full wp-image-1079394" /><br/>
The holiday 2026 Festivitas update adds new customizations. For the first time, you can upload custom bulb shapes. There's an option to import an image and convert it to a bulb in a string of lights. Simple PNGs on a transparent background produce the best results, and for more complex images, bulbs can use the original colors.<br/>
<br/>
You can customize light shapes, color, size, opacity, drop height, cable thickness, and more in the Settings. Lights can animate with different patterns, like pulse, twinkle, chase, and wave.<br/>
<br/>
There are pointer decorations like sparkles, bubbles, falling leaves, confetti, a Christmas tree, a wreath, and more. Falling snow will now pile up on top of app windows for an added holiday effect. You can adjust snow rate, density, and flake size.<br/>
<br/>
Snow and lights can be displayed above app windows or just on the desktop, with lights also visible on the Dock. Shortcuts can be used to play music and turn on lights, activate the snow effect if the weather forecast includes snow, and start a snowstorm.<br/>
<br/>
Along with custom bulb shapes and falling snow, Festivitas for the iPhone and &zwnj;iPad&zwnj; now fully transparent animated widgets on the without a screenshot workaround.<br/>
<br/>
Festivitas for Mac requires some accessibility access to work. I asked Støvring about Apple's planned permission changes in macOS 27.3, and how they will affect the Festivitas app. He said Festivitas gets far more access than it needs because Apple's options aren't granular enough, and he'd prefer to ask for less access.<br/>
<br/>
Menu bar lights, snow, and pointer effects work without the System &amp; App Protection Override permission, while lights in the Dock and snow landing on windows require access.<br/>
<br/>
